#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:2
msgid "Test snaps with snap try"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:4
msgid ""
"The `snap try` command installs a snap from its unpackaged components within "
"a directory. After installation, most changes to the components in that "
"directory will immediately by visible in the installed snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:6
msgid ""
"Ordinarily, snaps cannot be modified. They are distributed as, and executed "
"from, read-only SquashFS files whose integrity is guaranteed via {ref}"
"`assertions <explanation-security-assertions>` and the store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:8
msgid ""
"But it’s sometimes useful to be able to experiment with a snap locally, to "
"help debug an issue, or to make changes to a snap when you don’t have "
"upstream access to the packaging process. This is when `snap try` is useful."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:10
msgid ""
"If you only have access to the packaged snap itself, you first need to "
"_unsquash_ the snap and then use `snap try` to test it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:11
msgid ""
"If, instead, you are a developer with access to the source code of the snap, "
"you first need to use `snapcraft try` to build an unpacked snap, and then "
"use `snap try` to test it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:13
msgid "Unsquashing a snap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:15
msgid ""
"In order to use `snap try`, you first need to get the unpacked contents of "
"the snap. The first step is to get the snap itself. The most practical "
"source of snaps is the snap store, where a snap can be downloaded with the "
"`snap download` command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:17
msgid ""
"$ snap download hello-world\n"
"Fetching snap \"hello-world\"\n"
"Fetching assertions for \"hello-world\"\n"
"Install the snap with:\n"
"snap ack hello-world_29.assert\n"
"snap install hello-world_29.snap\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:26
msgid ""
"The download includes the snap itself and a signed set of {ref}`assertions "
"<explanation-security-assertions>` from the store to validate the snap’s "
"default state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:28
msgid ""
"Alternatively, _.snap_ files for any installed snaps can be found at `/var/"
"lib/snapd/snaps/`, from where they can be copied across to your current "
"working directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:30
msgid ""
"To uncompress the SquashFS _.snap_ file, use `unsquashfs <snap filename>`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:32
msgid ""
"$ unsquashfs hello-world_29.snap\n"
"Parallel unsquashfs: Using 8 processors\n"
"6 inodes (6 blocks) to write\n"
"[===========================|] 6/6 100%\n"
"created 6 files\n"
"created 4 directories\n"
"created 0 symlinks\n"
"created 0 device\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:43
msgid ""
"The files associated with the snap can now be found in the `squashfs-root’ "
"directory. You can use `snap try` to install this unpacked snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:45
msgid "Using snap try"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:47
msgid ""
"Running `snap try <directory>` installs an unpacked snap using a bind mount."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:49
msgid ""
"$ snap try squashfs-root\n"
"hello-world 6.4 mounted from /home/user/squashfs-root\n"
"$ which hello-world\n"
"/snap/bin/hello-world\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:56
msgid ""
"Most changes now made to files in the `squashfs-root` folder will be "
"immediately reflected in the installed snap. This can be helpful when "
"debugging an application within a snap, or the snap itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:58
msgid ""
"ⓘ Certain changes, such as adjusting a snap’s interfaces or confinement will "
"not be reflected in the installed snap until after a reinstall. These "
"changes include:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:60
msgid "changes to snap interfaces, such as adding or removing a plug"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:61
msgid "changes to layouts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:62
msgid "changes to a snap’s confinement"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:64
msgid ""
"Using the above hello-world snap, for example, we could edit the `bin/echo` "
"script to change its output without rebuilding or remounting the snap:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:66
msgid ""
"$ hello-world\n"
"Hello world!\n"
"$ sed -i 's/World/Everyone/g' /home/user/squashfs-root/bin/echo\n"
"$ hello-world\n"
"Hello Everyone!\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:74
msgid "Using snapcraft try"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:76
msgid ""
"When developing a snap with [snapcraft](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/"
"snapcraft/stable/), the `snapcraft try` command can be used in combination "
"with `snap try` to quickly test a snap and fix issues."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:78
msgid ""
"The `snapcraft try` command runs through the build process to the completion "
"of the _prime_ stage (see [Parts lifecycle](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/"
"snapcraft/stable/explanation/parts-lifecycle/) for further details). It then "
"exposes the resultant _prime_ directory to the snapcraft directory, even "
"from within a virtual machine or container."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:80
msgid ""
"This _prime_ directory includes all the staged components of a snap, which "
"can then be installed and tested with the `snap try <prime directory>` "
"command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:82
msgid ""
"The following,  example, will build a _hello-world_ snap within [LXD]"
"(https://canonical.com/lxd) and offer its _prime_ directory locally:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:84
msgid ""
"\n"
"$ snapcraft try --use-lxd\n"
"Pulling hello-world\n"
"+ snapcraftctl pull\n"
"Building hello-world\n"
"+ snapcraftctl build\n"
"+ cp --archive --link --no-dereference . /root/parts/hello-world/install\n"
"Staging hello-world\n"
"+ snapcraftctl stage\n"
"Priming hello-world\n"
"+ snapcraftctl prime\n"
"You can now run `snap try /home/user/hello-world/prime`.\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:99
msgid ""
"The above snap can then be installed and tested with `snap try` and the "
"_prime_ directory as its target:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:101
msgid ""
"$ snap try /home/user/hello-world/prime\n"
"hello-world 0.1 mounted from /home/user/hello-world/prime\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/snap-try.md:106
msgid ""
"For further help on testing and debugging a snap, see [Iterating over a "
"build](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/stable/how-to/debugging/"
"debug-a-snap/) and {ref}`Debugging snaps <how-to-guides-fix-common-issues-"
"debug-snaps>`."
msgstr ""
